// Observability-only. Like the sibling `runtime` and `health` blocks it never
// reflects or drives the lifecycle `state` machine, and it may lag — read
// `observedAt` to judge staleness.
//
// **Read `state` first.** It is a single value that answers "is the control
// plane ready", and `message` answers "why not" for every value that is not
// `ready`. The counters beside them are the evidence behind that judgement,
// published so a client can show detail without deriving the judgement itself.
// Nothing here requires a second request or a calculation.
//
// **Presence is the signal.** The block is omitted entirely when there is no
// hosted control plane to observe (a standalone cluster), when the watcher has
// not reported yet, or when what it reported was not a complete observation —
// every field listed as required must have been observed, or nothing is
// published. It is also omitted in the rare case where the observation could
// not be brought within its storage size limit at all, even after the shedding
// described under `message`. That says nothing about the control plane. It is
// never emitted partially populated: a zero-valued summary is indistinguishable
// from a genuine "control plane is down" reading, so the only safe answer to
// "we did not observe this" is to omit the block. A client must therefore test
// for the block's presence, and must NOT read its absence as a set of zeros.
