// VPCSecurityGroupsRequest Request to set the security groups attached to a VPC.
//
// **Replace, not merge.** The list sent becomes the VPC's whole binding: ids
// present are attached, ids previously attached and now absent are detached.
// Re-sending the same body converges on the same state, so a retry is safe.
//
// **A VPC always carries at least one group.** Sending `[]` does NOT leave the
// VPC unfiltered — it reinstates the VPC's platform-managed default group, and
// the response echoes that group like any other binding. So a tenant who clears
// their own groups can see what they are now filtered by, instead of reading an
// empty list and assuming nothing applies. A VPC with no default group to fall
// back to refuses `[]` with `409`.
//
// To move off the default, attach your own group: the default is replaced like
// any other entry. It survives being replaced and is still not deletable.
//
// `securityGroups` is required. Omitting the field is rejected with `422` rather
// than being read as "change nothing", so a misspelled or dropped field can
// never silently change a VPC's security posture.
//
// **The binding reaches the fabric through the VPC's owner, but the VPC
// reports the outcome.** These groups are rendered onto the NetworkBundles of
// the cluster or instance group that materialized the VPC. The POST returns
// `202` with this VPC in `updating`; poll THIS VPC's `state` (not the owner)
// until it settles `active`, or `failed` with `stateMessage` if the render did
// not land. The owner itself is not moved transitional by this call. See the
// `securityGroups` field on `VPC`.
type VPCSecurityGroupsRequest struct {
	// SecurityGroups Ids of the security groups to attach, replacing any current attachment.
	// Every id must name a security group that exists in the same project;
	// an unknown id fails the whole request with `422` and no partial binding
	// is written. Duplicates are rejected rather than collapsed. Order is
	// significant and round-trips: the list reaches the fabric as sent, after
	// the groups of the cluster or instance group that owns this VPC, which
	// therefore take precedence over these.
	//
	// This VPC's OWN default group may be named here, and doing so is how you
	// keep the platform-managed floor while adding groups of your own: attaching
	// only your own groups drops the default out of the binding. Sending `[]`
	// reinstates the default on its own.
	//
	// Another VPC's default group is refused with `422`. Its rules are derived
	// from that VPC's subnet prefixes, so on this VPC they would describe the
	// wrong network's perimeter — and it is removed when its own VPC is, which
	// would silently shrink this VPC's filter set. A default group is attachable
	// only to the VPC that owns it.
	//
	// At most 16 groups, matching the limit on a cluster's and an instance
	// group's binding. The two limits add up rather than compete: the rules
	// filtering one network interface are those of every group on this VPC
	// *plus* those of every group on the cluster or instance group that owns
	// it. Sized together with the 256-rule per-group limit so that combined
	// set stays within the fabric's own ceiling on rules per interface.
	SecurityGroups []string `json:"securityGroups"`
}

